# conformance/vibenvm/nvm-tests.allow — the subset of the pinned nvm-sh's own
# test suite that may be run against `vibenvm`, and nothing more.
#
# Authority: PROP-075 `#VIBENVM-SUITE`. "Reusing nvm's own tests" is a bonus,
# never the gate: nvm's `test/fast/` suite is *near*-offline — it stubs
# `nvm_download` and compares against recorded mocks in
# `test/fast/Unit tests/mocks/` — but it has no suite-wide mirror override and
# no local fixture server, and a few of its tests reach `nodejs.org`. So the
# reuse is bounded to this recorded list, every line of which is a test that
#   (a) exercises only the public `nvm` function, never an `nvm_*` internal,
#   (b) needs no network at all, and
#   (c) asserts behaviour `#VIBENVM-MAPPING` marks `maps`, not an
#       owner-approved exclusion and not nvm's own internal layout.
#
# Running them needs `urchin` (nvm's test runner) and the pinned tree:
#     cd conformance/vibenvm/mirror/nvm && urchin -f "test/fast/<name>"
# with the sandbox env `run.sh` builds. `urchin` is not a prerequisite of the
# suite and is absent on the machine this file was written on, so the reuse is
# recorded here and is not part of any verdict.
#
# Lines are paths inside the pinned tree. One per line; `#` is a comment.

test/fast/Running 'nvm exec' and 'nvm run' treat digit-named files as arguments, not versions
test/fast/Running 'nvm exec' and 'nvm run' without a resolvable version warn and fall back
test/fast/Running 'nvm install' with an invalid version fails nicely
test/fast/Running 'nvm' should not change or export the caller's IFS
test/fast/Running 'nvm use' and 'nvm deactivate' with hashing disabled
test/fast/Running 'nvm use' does not leave a stale hashed node
test/fast/Running 'nvm use x' should create and change the 'current' symlink
test/fast/Running 'nvm use x' should not create the 'current' symlink if $NVM_SYMLINK_CURRENT is false

# --- out of scope, and why -----------------------------------------------
#
# Whole directories, by construction (`#VIBENVM-SUITE` lists them): `slow/`,
# `installation_node/`, `installation_iojs/`, `sourcing/`, `install_script/`
# and `xenial/` all need the network or install nvm itself.
#
# `test/fast/Unit tests/**` — every one of them calls an `nvm_*` internal
#   (`nvm_download`, `nvm_alias`, `nvm_ls_remote`, `nvm_get_checksum`, …).
#   The shim has no internals to call: `#VIBENVM` makes it a mapping onto
#   vibe commands that holds no version logic of its own.
#
# `test/fast/Aliases/**`, and the top-level `nvm alias` / `nvm unalias` /
#   `set-colors` tests — named user aliases, colour setting: owner-approved
#   exclusions (`#VIBENVM-EXCLUSIONS-APPROVED`), so there is nothing to
#   compare.
#
# The `iojs` and `system` tests — io.js is a dead product and `system` is the
#   slot off-switch rather than a version; both are mapping decisions, not
#   behaviours to reproduce.
#
# `nvm --help documents …`, `nvm uninstall … shows the … message` — these
#   assert nvm's own wording. The shim's help is its own text generated from
#   the mapping table, and the suite compares observation records rather than
#   output text, so asserting nvm's sentences would be asserting the wrong
#   thing.
#
# `Running 'nvm-exec' …` — the `nvm-exec` helper script, not the `nvm`
#   function.
#
# `Sourcing nvm.sh …`, `nvm should remove the last trailing slash in
#   $NVM_DIR`, `nvm deactivate should unset the nvm environment variables`,
#   `nvm unload should unset all function and variables` — the mechanics of
#   nvm's own file and its own variable names. `$NVM_DIR` maps to vibe's
#   install root and `unload` maps to `vibe app env --unset`, but the
#   variables these tests name are nvm's internal state, which the owner
#   approved as an exclusion.
